Flagged in a Linus-style review: the exact O(N) subprocess-per-commit anti-pattern fixed in git-notes-grep.sh (ac556dd) had been reintroduced, unnoticed, in its two sibling scripts. Both walked commit history and forked `git notes show` per commit.

git-why-record.sh's version was the worse of the two: its loop only breaks early once it finds the first noted commit, so on a repo with zero notes fetched -- the exact state the README's own push/fetch caveat describes as the default after a plain clone -- it never breaks, and forks git once per commit across the *entire* history just to conclude "nothing found." Verified by cloning this repo without its notes ref and timing the before/after: confirmed the old code path would have forked per commit; the fix (build the noted-commit set with one `git notes list` call, then test membership as a pure bash substring check `[[ "$noted" == *$'\n'"$sha"$'\n'* ]]`) produces the same output with zero forks in that same scenario.

git-why.sh needed a different shape of fix because, unlike the record script, it needs actual note *content*, not just a yes/no. Built two parallel arrays from one `git notes list` call (commit shas and their note blobs -- no `declare -A`, same bash-3.2 constraint as everywhere else in this repo), then for each commit touching the file, do a bash-only linear scan for a match before ever forking `git show`. Most commits touching a given file don't have a note, so this still turns "one fork per commit in the file's history" into "zero forks for non-matches, one `git show` per actual match" -- same principle as the record-script fix, adapted because this script's job is retrieval, not just detection.
